.TH ed 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
ed \- the line editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ed
[\fB\-\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ed
edits text a line at a time.  It has no screen and no cursor: you name
lines by number or by what they contain, and it does what you ask.  On a
serial console with no terminal type set, it is the editor that always
works.
.PP
.B ed
is quiet.  It answers a mistake with a
.B ?
and nothing else.  Type
.B P
to have it prompt with a
.BR * .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-
Do not print the character counts after reading and writing.
.TP
.B \-q
Let the quit signal through, and print counts.
.SH ADDRESSES
.TP
.I n
Line
.IR n .
.TP
.B .
The current line.
.TP
.B $
The last line.
.TP
.BI / re /
The next line matching
.IR re ,
wrapping round the end.
.TP
.BI ? re ?
The previous line matching it.
.TP
.BI ' x
The line marked
.I x
by the
.B k
command.
.TP
.BI + n
Forward from the current line.
.TP
.BI \- n
Back from the current line.
.TP
.B ,
.B 1,$
\- the whole file.
.PP
An address range is two addresses separated by a comma.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B a
Append text after the addressed line.  A line holding only a dot ends
the input.
.TP
.B i
Insert text before it.
.TP
.B c
Replace the addressed lines with typed text.
.TP
.B d
Delete the addressed lines.
.TP
.B m
Move the addressed lines to after another address.
.TP
.B t
Copy them there instead.
.TP
.B j
Join the addressed lines into one.
.TP
.B p
Print them.
.TP
.B l
Print them with non-printing characters shown.
.TP
.B P
Turn the
.B *
prompt on or off.
.TP
.BI k x
Mark the addressed line
.IR x .
.TP
.BI s/ re / replacement /
Substitute on the addressed lines.  A trailing
.B g
changes every occurrence on the line, a trailing
.B p
prints the result.
.TP
.BI g/ re / commands
Run
.I commands
on every line matching
.IR re .
.TP
.BI v/ re / commands
The same for lines
.I not
matching.
.TP
.B u
Undo the last change.  One level only.
.TP
.BI e " file"
Start again on
.IR file .
.B E
does it even if the buffer has unsaved changes.
.TP
.BI r " file"
Read
.I file
in after the addressed line.
.TP
.BI w " file"
Write the addressed lines, the whole buffer by default, to
.I file
or to the current file name.
.B W
appends instead.
.TP
.BI f " file"
Show, or set, the current file name.
.TP
.B =
Print the line number of the address.
.TP
.BI ! " command"
Run a shell command.
.TP
.B q
Quit.  It refuses once if there are unsaved changes;
.B Q
does not ask at all.
.PP
A bare newline prints the next line.
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
.BR . ,
.BR * ,
.BR [ ... ] ,
.B ^
and
.B $
at the ends, and
.BR \\( ... \\)
with
.BR \\1 " to " \\9
in the replacement.
.B &
in a replacement stands for the whole match.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/e*
The edit buffer.  A file being edited is held here, not in memory, so
the size of what you can edit is a question about
.B /tmp
rather than about the process.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# ed prog.bas
a
10 PRINT "hello"
\&.
w
q
.fi
.SH NOTES
Two editors on this machine are easier to get on with:
.BR vi (1)
for C and text, and
.BR mmedit (1)
for BASIC.  Keep
.B ed
for scripts, and for the day the screen handling will not cooperate.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vi (1),
.BR mmedit (1),
.BR sed (1)
